    "report": "Phone numbers written with hyphens are now recognised as contact details. The change is committed on `main` as `6cb6a62`, not pushed. No server was started.\n\n**What changed**\n- The phone rule is now `\\+?\\d(?:[ .-]?\\d){8,}` in two places, matching ADR 0063's note that each context holding the rule changes it:\n  - `src/Contexts/Messaging/Domain/Conversation/ContactDetails.ts`: messages hold these numbers back as `[hidden]`.\n  - `src/Contexts/Reviews/Domain/StayReview/ReviewRules.ts`: reviews and replies refuse them as `CONTACT_DETAILS`.\n- Specs added in both contexts. They cover `06-12-34-56-78` and `+33-6-12-34-56-78`, and check that `2026-12-01` and `06-12-34-56` are not phone numbers.\n\n**Checks**\n- All 1030 unit tests pass.\n- Prettier, ESLint, typecheck and `check:conventions` all pass.\n- I did not run the e2e suite.\n\n**Decisions and open points**\n- Committed on `main` because you asked for the current branch. The git guidance I follow normally says to branch off the default branch first, so say if you'd rather have a branch.\n- Two dates separated only by a space or hyphen, such as `2026-11-20 2026-11-25`, now chain into one 14-digit run and count as a phone number. Dots and spaces already did this before my change. I kept the rule literal rather than carve out dates.\n- I left ADRs 0055 and 0063 unchanged. They describe the rule generally, and ADRs are historical records.",
